// CONFORMANCE-CASE
// spec: 14-control-flow
// facility: rule.control.block [Ref-Binding-Last-Use] (the deferred end, D-0221, CHG-0260); conf.ref-holding-binding-end-deferred
// kind: positive
// expect: ok
// stdout: 2
//
// `h1` copies `r1`'s path. `r1` is last mentioned in `h1`'s declaration,
// but it shares that statement with `h1`, a struct holding a reference,
// which is mentioned after it, so its end waits until `h1` is no longer
// mentioned: both end after `*h1.r = *h1.r + 1`, and reading `y` meets no
// live exclusive path. The text before D-0221 held such a binding to its
// block's exit, and the read faulted; both implementations always deferred
// (D-0219 finding 8). Before D-0275 the third statement was
// `*r1 = *r1 + 1`, with `h1` waiting for `r1`, a reference to an `i32`,
// which no longer holds anything past its last mention.

import std;

struct Hold
{
    ref<i32, exclusive> r;
}

fn main()
{
    i32 y = 1;
    auto r1 = &mut y;
    Hold h1 = Hold { .r = r1 };
    *h1.r = *h1.r + 1;
    printf("%v\n", y);
}
